+# Batch size
+
+`BATCH` in `sync.sh` is **200**, in place since 2026-09-18 (counted back from the
+four clean days recorded below). Do not raise it on the strength of the vendor's
+documented limit. That limit is not the binding constraint; a gateway timeout is,
+and the timeout appears in nothing the vendor publishes.
+
+## The rule
+
+- Leave `BATCH` at 200 unless a per-call duration measurement says otherwise
+  (see "What would settle it").
+- `/v2/ingest` accepts up to 1000 records per call. That number bounds payload
+  size, not call duration. Moving toward it makes each call longer, which is the
+  direction the evidence says hurts.
+- A change here is only testable in production: the failure is in the vendor's
+  edge, on their shared tier, and there is no staging equivalent.
+
+## What is established
+
+- Every failed cron run in the month before 2026-09-18 ran with `BATCH=500`.
+- Those failures were bare `502`s from the vendor's edge, with no response body.
+- The vendor's status page showed no incident in any of the failure windows.
+- With `BATCH=200`, no run failed in the four days after the change.
+
+## What the vendor said
+
+From a support call with Raj during the incident, shortly before `BATCH` was
+lowered. Secondhand, never independently confirmed:
+
+- Our account is on their shared tier.
+- There is a 30-second gateway timeout in front of `/v2/ingest`.
+- 500 records "usually" runs longer than that for accounts on that tier.
+
+This is the only account anyone has of *why* the `502`s happen. It fits the
+evidence, and nothing contradicts it, but it is an explanation we were handed,
+not one we reproduced.
+
+## What is not established
+
+- The mechanism. We never reproduced a failure at 500 after the call, so the
+  chain "500 records → call exceeds 30s → edge returns a bodyless 502" is
+  plausible and untested.
+- That 200 is the right number. It was not derived from a measurement. It is a
+  round value below 500 that stopped the failures.
+- That 200 has margin. No per-call duration has ever been measured at any batch
+  size, so how close 200 runs to the 30-second limit is unknown.
+- That four clean days means much. The prior failure rate was never quantified,
+  so the observation window may be shorter than the gap between failures.
+
+## Recognising a recurrence
+
+The failure signature — bodyless `502`, clean status page — is what a gateway
+timeout looks like from our side, and it is indistinguishable from any other
+fault in their edge. So a `502` at `BATCH=200` is ambiguous on its own: it means
+either that per-call duration has grown past the limit (rows got bigger, or the
+tier got slower) or that batch size was never the cause. Lowering `BATCH` again
+without measuring would repeat the same guess.
+
+## What would settle it
+
+`sync.sh` records nothing about how long a call takes, which is why the incident
+ended in an inference rather than a finding. Capturing per-call wall time (e.g.
+`curl -w '%{time_total}'` into a log) would turn the next occurrence into
+evidence, and would answer the open question directly: if calls at 200 land well
+under 30s, the headroom is real and a higher `BATCH` can be argued for with
+numbers; if they land near it, 200 is already marginal.
